[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d68af87f-be02-55a7-9b78-0da04db4e309"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d68af87f-be02-55a7-9b78-0da04db4e309" :question/id #uuid "d68af87f-be02-55a7-9b78-0da04db4e309" :question/math-academy-id "q-79345" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Find $\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C)).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C))=2+1+1=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f77f73c8-a6f1-5686-8eae-81626a3ac1d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f77f73c8-a6f1-5686-8eae-81626a3ac1d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "f77f73c8-a6f1-5686-8eae-81626a3ac1d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-787219f1-94ee-53fb-a49b-8777fcdb7957" :answer-field/choices ["answer-75415968-8129-50a3-b6ce-101666b52758" "answer-982cdb38-b85b-58e9-908